[1] (about=User_2, episodic, t109 @2025-07-21T08:38:29) Yes — keep hours as the single SLA control, and let queue volume sit in the memo as operational impact. That keeps audit clean and avoids two competing boundaries during validation.  

@Model Risk can keep the back-test pack banded by typology with sample size, lift, false-positive drift, and rationale notes only where bands materially change. @Operations, please confirm the hour cutoff by Friday against http://sharepoint/aml_(anti-money_laundering)_project/alert_scoring_model_design_spec so we can freeze one definition for validation.
[2] (about=User_2, episodic, t111 @2025-07-21T18:30:43) @Model Risk @Operations Given the typology retest results, I think we should revise the validation approach slightly and treat queue volume as a secondary control rather than memo-only. The original hours-only decision no longer holds: queue volume is varying materially by alert type at the same cutoff, and one high-volume band is already outside the expected validation range even though SLA hours look stable.  

**Proposal:** keep hours as the primary SLA control, add queue volume as a secondary checkpoint with an explicit tolerance band, and trigger revalidation if that band is breached. This is cleaner than memo-only because it reflects actual investigator prioritization and captures operational impact without creating two competing SLA definitions.  

@Operations @Model Risk please confirm the tolerance band and revalidation trigger by Friday so we can update the validation spec and lock it.
